1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,112
Hi, I'm Suketh Produtoor.

2
00:00:02,132 --> 00:00:06,428
I'm an international student on F-1, looking for new-grad machine learning roles.

3
00:00:06,448 --> 00:00:09,465
Before I tailor an application, I need to know one thing:

4
00:00:09,485 --> 00:00:11,063
does this posting rule me out?

5
00:00:11,083 --> 00:00:12,150
Titles can't tell me.

6
00:00:12,170 --> 00:00:16,288
Earlier this term, a recipe Claude Code and I built screened eight real postings

7
00:00:16,308 --> 00:00:19,082
for a fictional new grad, by title and sponsor record.

8
00:00:19,102 --> 00:00:21,048
When an AI reviewer read them in full,

9
00:00:21,068 --> 00:00:25,186
six asked for two to twelve-plus years of experience, or excluded new graduates.

10
00:00:25,208 --> 00:00:26,637
So I'm building Fine Print.

11
00:00:26,657 --> 00:00:28,247
It will read each new posting,

12
00:00:28,267 --> 00:00:32,272
show me the exact sentence behind every requirement that could rule me out,

13
00:00:32,292 --> 00:00:35,599
and wait for my approval before anything reaches my shortlist.

14
00:00:35,625 --> 00:00:36,893
And postings keep coming.

15
00:00:36,913 --> 00:00:40,756
In under two weeks, the three boards I watch showed one hundred seventy-two

16
00:00:40,776 --> 00:00:43,486
postings that weren't in my November eighth snapshot.

17
00:00:43,506 --> 00:00:46,061
One piece of Fine Print already runs: the watcher.

18
00:00:46,081 --> 00:00:47,040
The reader is next.

19
00:00:47,083 --> 00:00:48,156
What already exists?

20
00:00:48,176 --> 00:00:52,306
My own first try matched on title and sponsor record, and couldn't tell fit.

21
00:00:52,326 --> 00:00:56,347
On LinkedIn, I checked two graduate ML postings, from Qualcomm and TikTok.

22
00:00:56,367 --> 00:00:59,242
Both separated minimum from preferred qualifications.

23
00:00:59,262 --> 00:01:00,716
Neither stated sponsorship.

24
00:01:00,750 --> 00:01:02,097
Others have built pieces.

25
00:01:02,117 --> 00:01:05,871
Government H-1B data counts an employer's past approvals and denials,

26
00:01:05,891 --> 00:01:07,402
not what a posting asks for.

27
00:01:07,422 --> 00:01:11,176
Jobscan scores a résumé against a posting and lists missing keywords.

28
00:01:11,196 --> 00:01:15,278
One paper flags whether experience and education are required or preferred.

29
00:01:15,298 --> 00:01:19,653
And a 2024 study found language models find skills but miss their exact wording.

30
00:01:19,708 --> 00:01:23,438
Fine Print will put those pieces together for one question: should I apply?

31
00:01:23,458 --> 00:01:26,188
Each requirement will carry the posting's own sentence,

32
00:01:26,208 --> 00:01:27,688
checked word for word by code.

33
00:01:27,708 --> 00:01:31,788
Required will stay apart from preferred, silence on sponsorship will mean unknown,

34
00:01:31,808 --> 00:01:34,387
and nothing will reach my shortlist until I approve.

35
00:01:34,417 --> 00:01:34,851
Now, how.

36
00:01:34,871 --> 00:01:37,226
In October I planned to build the reader first,

37
00:01:37,246 --> 00:01:41,420
and now I can build it on top of jobwatch, which already monitors three job boards.

38
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:44,604
Its data: public job-board feeds, read only from an allow-list,

39
00:01:44,624 --> 00:01:47,837
each response saved with a fingerprint before anything reads it.

40
00:01:47,857 --> 00:01:49,202
The applicant is fictional.

41
00:01:49,250 --> 00:01:52,487
On November twenty-first, Claude Code ran it from a fresh copy.

42
00:01:52,507 --> 00:01:54,555
All one hundred sixty-four tests passed.

43
00:01:54,575 --> 00:01:58,070
Then a live run read three boards, Anthropic, Databricks and Linear,

44
00:01:58,090 --> 00:02:02,051
and found one hundred seventy-two postings it hadn't seen on November eighth.

45
00:02:02,083 --> 00:02:04,296
Its simple word rule, from my first agent,

46
00:02:04,316 --> 00:02:07,752
rejected all one hundred seventy-two for the fictional applicant.

47
00:02:07,772 --> 00:02:09,241
Were those rejections right?

48
00:02:09,261 --> 00:02:10,410
A word rule can't say.

49
00:02:10,430 --> 00:02:12,324
That's the gap the reader will fill.

50
00:02:12,375 --> 00:02:16,232
The run also refused to save its memory, because I hadn't approved that:

51
00:02:16,252 --> 00:02:18,224
it ended incomplete, state unchanged.

52
00:02:18,244 --> 00:02:22,262
On November eighth, I typed that approval myself, for one run and one file,

53
00:02:22,282 --> 00:02:23,662
and only then did it save.

54
00:02:23,708 --> 00:02:27,651
So far, in both job-search agents, I wrote the rules first, before any code.

55
00:02:27,671 --> 00:02:31,561
Claude Code wrote the code, all but five of the tests, and ran most checks.

56
00:02:31,581 --> 00:02:35,784
I ran my own checks, and twice I withheld my sign-off until the record was right.

57
00:02:35,833 --> 00:02:36,792
Next, step by step.

58
00:02:36,812 --> 00:02:40,293
By November twenty-fifth, the reader: each claim labelled supported,

59
00:02:40,313 --> 00:02:44,156
contradicted or insufficient by the posting's own text, or 'I cannot tell'.

60
00:02:44,176 --> 00:02:47,503
By December first, every quote checked against the saved posting.

61
00:02:47,523 --> 00:02:50,799
By December thirteenth, my approval tied to the exact shortlist.

62
00:02:50,819 --> 00:02:52,858
December twentieth: the full evaluation.

63
00:02:52,917 --> 00:02:54,052
Do I have what I need?

64
00:02:54,072 --> 00:02:57,467
No API credits: model calls will go through my Claude Code login,

65
00:02:57,487 --> 00:03:00,986
as my first agent's judge did for twenty-seven postings in October.

66
00:03:01,006 --> 00:03:03,560
Public boards need no accounts; a laptop runs it.

67
00:03:03,580 --> 00:03:05,977
The limits: boards can refuse automated reads,

68
00:03:05,997 --> 00:03:08,236
and usage caps mean a daily reading budget.

69
00:03:08,292 --> 00:03:09,733
How will I know it worked?

70
00:03:09,753 --> 00:03:11,981
I'll label real public postings by hand,

71
00:03:12,001 --> 00:03:15,072
including a random sample of the ones my rule rejected,

72
00:03:15,092 --> 00:03:18,107
and lock a held-out group away before tuning anything.

73
00:03:18,167 --> 00:03:21,776
I'll measure four things: whether every quote really appears in the posting;

74
00:03:21,796 --> 00:03:25,072
misreads, like 'preferred' read as 'required'; two errors kept apart,

75
00:03:25,092 --> 00:03:27,794
clearing the applicant for a requirement they don't meet,

76
00:03:27,814 --> 00:03:31,758
and ruling out a posting they'd qualify for; and how often it says 'I cannot tell'.

77
00:03:31,792 --> 00:03:35,456
I'll break it on purpose at least five times: a quote with one word changed,

78
00:03:35,476 --> 00:03:38,994
an either-or rule, a posting silent on sponsorship, a board that refuses.

79
00:03:39,014 --> 00:03:42,145
And the failure I'm predicting now: a quote copied word for word,

80
00:03:42,165 --> 00:03:46,023
but 'five years preferred' treated as a requirement, caught by a separate check.

81
00:03:46,083 --> 00:03:50,021
It worked if at least twenty-seven of thirty held-out decisions match my labels and

82
00:03:50,041 --> 00:03:51,976
my checks catch every deliberate failure.

83
00:03:51,996 --> 00:03:54,313
It didn't work if the reader misquotes a posting,

84
00:03:54,333 --> 00:03:55,934
clears even one unmet requirement,

85
00:03:55,954 --> 00:03:58,414
or wrongly rules out more than one eligible posting,

86
00:03:58,434 --> 00:03:59,940
regardless of the overall score.

87
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:03,850
I'll label about sixty postings by hand, including thirty randomly picked rejects,

88
00:04:03,870 --> 00:04:05,519
and set thirty aside before tuning.

89
00:04:05,542 --> 00:04:08,822
One honest limit: a set this small can show me where it fails.

90
00:04:08,842 --> 00:04:11,005
It can't prove it works on every posting.

91
00:04:11,042 --> 00:04:11,802
Fine Print.

92
00:04:11,822 --> 00:04:13,433
Suketh Reddy Produtoor.
